# Scratch work, not the project.
#
# Everything here is UNSHIPPED: one-shot probes written to answer one
# question, mutation tables written to prove the suite bites, their captured
# output, and the UI sketches the build was designed against. Not part of the
# build, not something anybody should have to read, and they must never reach
# a commit.
#
# The convention is the leading underscore, and it is the whole rule: a file
# named _anything is scratch. Everything without one is the project. That rule
# is currently TOTAL rather than merely broad — every file on disk is either
# tracked or matched here, nothing falls between — and the one-line check for
# it is at the bottom of this file.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# UNSHIPPED IS NOT THE SAME AS DISPOSABLE. DO NOT BULK-DELETE BY THE PATTERN.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The underscore says "git does not carry this". It does not say "nobody needs
# this", and reading it as though it did is the mistake this paragraph exists
# to stop. Two of these files are load-bearing right now:
#
#   scripts/_mutate-fixes.mjs   THE LIVE MUTATION TABLE. Every fix from the
#                               audit list has rows in it, and it is how each
#                               one was shown to be caught rather than merely
#                               made to pass. Run before touching js/. Deleting
#                               it throws away the evidence for the whole list
#                               and there is no command that rebuilds it.
#
#   _sketch.html                Named in a comment inside itself at :719, which
#   scripts/_sketchcheck.mjs    runs its layout block headless. The pair is a
#                               cross-reference, so deleting one strands the
#                               other's explanation.
#
# The rest divide by what losing them would actually cost. The one-shot probes
# each answered a question that is now an assertion in scripts/test-engine.mjs,
# so they are cheap to lose and cheap to rewrite. The per-checkpoint mutation
# tables (_mutate-bot, _mutate-net, _mutate-shell, _mutate-ui, _mutate) are the
# expensive ones: they cover ground _mutate-fixes.mjs does not, and rewriting
# one is hours, not minutes.
#
# Keeping the scripts costs ~190kB that git never sees. That is the entire
# downside, and it is smaller than re-deriving any one of them.
#
# CAPTURED OUTPUT IS THE EXCEPTION, AND IT IS DELETED ON SIGHT. Redirected run
# logs — .out, .progress, .log — are not artefacts, they are a transcript of
# one execution of something that is still on disk and still runnable. Every
# line of a .out can be reproduced by re-running the table that printed it,
# which is the test for whether a file belongs in the paragraph above: if a
# command rebuilds it, keeping it buys nothing and costs a stale copy.
#
# The stale copy is the actual hazard, not the bytes. A _mutate-fixes.out sat
# here from a run four days older than the table beside it, and a capture that
# no longer matches what the harness prints is worse than no capture at all —
# it reads as evidence, and it is evidence for a version of the code that no
# longer exists. Nothing reads these files; they exist only to be glanced at.
# Glance, then delete.
#
# So: none are checked in here now, and none should accumulate. If you want a
# run kept, keep it somewhere it will be read, with the date and the commit it
# describes — not as a bare .out next to the script that outgrew it.
#
# NOT ignored, and deliberately so:
#   scripts/test-engine.mjs   the suite itself
#   scripts/domshim.mjs       the DOM the suite renders into, headless
#   scripts/peershim.mjs      the transport the suite talks over, headless
#   scripts/gen-icons.js      generates icons/, which ships
#
# That list is hand-written, so treat it as a description and not as the
# enforcement — a fifth keeper added to scripts/ will not appear in it on its
# own. What IS checkable is that nothing has fallen through the gap:
#
#     git ls-files --others --exclude-standard     # must print nothing
#
# Empty means every file on disk is either tracked or deliberately ignored. A
# line of output is either a new project file nobody staged or scratch work
# missing its underscore, and the two look identical until somebody commits.
_*

# ...but the shipped page is index.html, and no underscore file is loaded by
# it. If that ever stops being true, this pattern is the thing that hides it.

# Editor/session state. .claude/ holds per-machine launch settings and
# transcripts, neither of which belong to anybody else checking this out.
.claude/

# OS and editor litter.
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
*~

# There are no dependencies — package.json has no "dependencies" key and the
# page loads ES modules straight from js/. node_modules/ should never appear;
# it is listed so that if some tool creates one, it does not get committed by
# somebody who did not notice.
node_modules/
